## Classical Dynamics

### Hamilton-Jacobi / Lagrange Formalism

### The Mechanics of Cotangent Bundles

### $$\theta := p\ dq - \mathcal H(p,q,t)\ dt$$

### $$\omega := d\theta = dp\wedge dq - d\mathcal H \wedge dt$$

```math
\begin{aligned}
\omega|_\gamma &= \dot{p}\ dt\wedge dq + \dot{q}\ dp\wedge dt - \frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial p}dp\wedge dt - \frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial q}dq\wedge dt \\
        &= (-\dot{p}^i - \frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial q^i}) dq^i \wedge dt+  (\dot{q}^i - \frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial p^i}) dp^i\wedge dt
\end{aligned}
```

#### $$\omega|_\gamma = 0 \iff \gamma$$ satisfies the Hamilton-Jacobi Equations
```math
\begin{aligned}
\dot p &= -\frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial q} \\
\dot q &= \ \ \ \frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial p}
\end{aligned}
```

$$\iff \gamma:[0,t]\rightarrow T^*(M\oplus\R)  $$ is a *stationary curve* for the Action $$S_\mathcal H(\gamma):=\int_\gamma \theta$$.

### Legendre Transform
When $$\mathcal H$$ is convex in $$p$$, $$\forall \dot{q} \in T_q M\ \exists !\ p=p_{max}(\dot q)$$  satisfying $$\dot{q} = \frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial p}(p_{max},q,t)$$.  This defines the (involutive) Legendre Transform $$\mathcal L$$ of $$\mathcal H$$:
```math
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{L}(\dot q,q,t) &:= \max_p p\dot{q} - \mathcal H(p,q,t) = p_{max}(\dot q)\dot q - \mathcal H(p_{max}(\dot q),q,t) \\
S_\mathcal{L}(\pi(\gamma)) &= \int_{\pi(\gamma)} \mathcal{L}(\dot q, q, t)\ dt
\end{aligned}
```
is the *Lagrangian representation of the Action*, where $$\pi: T^*(M\oplus\Reals) \rightarrow M\oplus\Reals$$ is the projection operator $$(p,q,E,t)\mapsto (q,t)$$.

#### The (Meta)Principle of Least Action

The Principle of Least Action simply claims that the Classical Dynamics of Nature Itself tends to select trajectories that minimize $$S_\mathcal{L}$$.

In general, this claim is **false**. But the set stationary curves of $$S_\mathcal H$$ are always interesting to discover, and they are identical to the curves that leave $$S_\mathcal{L}$$ stationary.  Locally, **the differential equations for those stationary trajectories are identical**, and so $$S_\mathcal H = S_\mathcal{L}$$ on those curves.  In the Lagrangian Formulation, these covariant equations are known as the **Euler-Lagrange Equations** $$(d\mathcal{L}\wedge dt)|_{\pi(\gamma)} = 0:$$

```math
\frac{\partial \mathcal L}{\partial q} = \frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial \mathcal L}{\partial \dot q}
```

which is a second-order ODE in $$t \mapsto q(t)$$, so has $$2\dim M$$ initial conditions $$(\dot q_0, q_0)$$, just as with the contravariant Hamilton-Jacobi Equations.

The interesting aspect of $$S_\mathcal L(\pi\circ\gamma)$$ is when can uniquely define $$\pi\circ\gamma$$ implicitly based on the endpoints $$(q_0, t_0)$$ and $$(q_f, t_f)$$, so we need to transform this boundary-value problem into an initial-value problem. In other words, we must solve for a $$\dot q_0$$ that will hit the target $$(q_f, t_f)$$ with a stationary curve $$\gamma$$ which solves the Euler-Lagrange Equations. In this way we can think of $$S = S(q_0,t_0, q_f, t_f)$$ as a **transition function**, assuming it does not depend on the choice of stationary $$\gamma$$, and such a $$\gamma$$ actually exists in the Solution Space of smooth curves connecting the pair of transition points.

We can now define a unique "horizontal" lift $$A=\dot q\oplus \pi^{-1}:T_{q} M\oplus \Reals \rightarrow T_{q,t}^{\*}(M\oplus \Reals)$$ by assigning

```math
(\dot q, q,t)\mapsto (p_{max}(\dot q), q, \mathcal H(p_{max}(\dot q), q, t), t)\ .
```
Now we have at last, for **any "projected" smooth curve** $$\tilde\gamma:[0,t]\rightarrow M\oplus\R$$:

```math
\begin{aligned}

S_\mathcal{L}(\tilde\gamma) &= S_\mathcal H(A\circ \tilde\gamma) \ .
\end{aligned}
```

#### Quadratic Form Magic, part 1

When $$H(p,q,t)$$'s $$p$$-dependence (aka the Kinetic Energy component) is a non-degenerate, symmetric quadratic form, we may represent it as a metric $$[g_{ij}]: T^{\*}M\odot T^{\*}M\rightarrow\Reals$$
with inverse $$[g^{ij}]: TM\odot TM\rightarrow\Reals$$. The Legendre Transform relates them:

```math
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal H(p,q,t) &= \frac{1}{2}\ g_{ij}(q,t)\ p^ip^j + V(q,t) \implies\\
\mathcal{L}(q,\dot q, t) &= \frac{1}{2}\ g^{ij}(q,t)\dot{q}_i\dot{q}_j - V(q,t)\ .
\end{aligned}
```

The Levi-Civita Connection's Christoffel Symbols for $$g$$ are simply defined by the Kozsul Formula

```math

\Gamma^k_{ij} = \frac{1}{2} g^{ka}(\partial_i g_{ja} + \partial_j g_{ia} - \partial_a g_{ij})

```
with $$\partial_i := \frac{\partial}{\partial q^i}$$. The associated Covariant Derivative in local coordinates is
```math
\nabla_{a^i\partial_i} b^j\partial_j =a^i\partial_ib^j\partial_j + \Gamma_{ij}^k a^ib ^j
\partial_k
```

Correspondingly, the Riemann-Christoffel Curvature Tensor is

```math
R^{\rho }{}_{\sigma \mu \nu }=\partial _{\mu }\Gamma ^{\rho }{}_{\nu \sigma }-\partial _{\nu }\Gamma ^{\rho }{}_{\mu \sigma }+\Gamma ^{\rho }{}_{\mu \lambda }\Gamma ^{\lambda }{}_{\nu \sigma }-\Gamma ^{\rho }{}_{\nu \lambda }\Gamma ^{\lambda }{}_{\mu \sigma }
```


##### Lagrange Multipliers on $$\mathcal H$$ as Infinitesimal Translations on $$\mathcal L$$

Furthermore, if $$\mathcal H = \mathcal H\_B$$ has an additional component $$B(q,t)\in T_qM$$, ie a linear funtional on $$p\in T^{\*}_qM$$, we can **complete the square** and recompute $$\mathcal{L}_B$$ in terms of $$\mathcal L$$:

```math
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal H_B(p,q,t) &= \frac{1}{2}\ g_{ij}\ p^ip^j + B(q,t)_ip^i + V(q,t) \\
 &= \frac{1}{2}\ g_{ij}\ (p^i+g^{ik}B_k)(p^j+g^{jk}B_k) - \frac{1}{2}\ g_{ij} g^{im}g^{jn}B_nB_m + V(q,t)\\

&= \mathcal H(p+g^{-1}B, q, t) - \frac{1}{2} g^{ij}B_iB_j \implies\\
\mathcal{L}_B(q,\dot q, t) &= \max_p (p+g^{-1}B)\dot q - \mathcal H(p+g^{-1}B,q,t) - g^{ij}B_i \dot q_j + \frac{1}{2}\ g^{ij}(q,t)B_iB_j\\
&= \mathcal{L}(q,\dot q, t)- g^{ij}B_i\dot{q}_i + \frac{1}{2}\ g^{ij}B_iB_j(q,t) \ ,\\
&= \mathcal{L}(q,\dot{q}-B(q,t), t)
\end{aligned}
```

Here we see the connection between the Lagrange Multiplier $$B$$ on $$\mathcal H$$ and its equivalent expression as an infinitesimal deformation on $$\mathcal L$$.  We will contextualize $$B$$ in a variety of useful ways in the remainder. **Both of the last two expressions for $$\mathcal{L}_B$$ in Equation <span class="eqno"></span> are critical**.

##### The Connection 1-form $$A$$ is explicit

Since $$\frac{\partial \mathcal H}{\partial p^j}(p,q,t) = g_{ij}p^i \implies \frac{\partial^2\mathcal H}{\partial p^i \partial p^j} = g_{ij}$$, we can compute the horizontal lift explicitly

```math
\begin{aligned}
p_{max}^i &= g^{ij}\dot q_i \implies \\
A(\dot q, q, t) &= (g^{-1}\dot q, q, \mathcal H(g^{-1}\dot q, q, t), t)\ .
\end{aligned}
```

When $$g_{ij}$$ is positive-definite, so is its inverse, which implies $$S_\mathcal L$$ is actually **minimized** on stationary curves.
